#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""berkeDark's installer: one command for every surface berkeDark covers.

    ./berkedark install [TARGET ...]     install the targets (all when none is named)
    ./berkedark uninstall [TARGET ...]   take berkeDark out again: files it placed go, the keys it set go back
    ./berkedark status                   what berkeDark has installed, file by file
    ./berkedark settings                 create your settings file, with commented examples
    ./berkedark qwen-diff-patch [ARGS]   the separate, explicit patch for Qwen Code's diff blocks
                                         (ARGS: --dry-run, --restore, --root DIR; see cli/README.md)

    --dry-run (with install or uninstall) says what would change and changes nothing.

Targets: terminal (foot's palette and berkeDark's colour interpreter), claude (Claude Code's theme), qwen
(Qwen Code's theme), codex (Codex's theme), grok (Grok's settings), ohmyzsh (the prompt), vscode (a note:
the VS Code theme comes from the Marketplace).

berkeDark is a thing that fixes every surface on its own: where a tool gives a theme no role, berkeDark
supplies the remedy. This installer only places berkeDark's files and sets the few settings that switch them
on. For every file it touches it keeps the file as it was (once, in ~/.local/state/berkeDark), changes in a
settings file only the keys berkeDark owns, checks the result, and can take everything out again: a settings
file gets back only berkeDark's keys as they were, so what you or the tool changed there since stays.

Your settings file, ~/.config/berkeDark/berkeDark.toml, may change colours of berkeDark's pool (the single
source of all its colours); every install writes berkeDark's files from the pool with your changes first.
Needs Python 3.11 or later; the terminal target needs clang and runs on Linux.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

